  "query_preview": "..\nThe atmosphere is primed for another day of scattered\nstrong to severe thunderstorms this afternoon and evening. An\nelongated ridge is in place with modest mid-level flow of 20-30\nkts at 500 mb. This will lead to 0-6 km shear of 25-35 kts with\nCAPE values 1000-1800 J/kg and seasonal PWAT values. Hi-res \nguidance shows initial development over the White Mountains and \nSky Islands east of Tucson by mid-afternoon. Current satellite\nshows cumulus development over northern Greenlee County. Storms\nthen progress westward into the lower elevations of Santa Cruz,\nCochise, and eastern Pima Counties late afternoon into the \nevening. Tucson will likely be the northern extent of storm \ncoverage. Heavy rainfall will be a threat in Cochise and Santa \nCruz Counties with HREF one inch in one hour probabilities \nwithin 25 km of a point 20-50 percent between 4PM - 10PM. Areas\nthat received heavy rain yesterday will also be more likely to \nsee runoff and flash flooding. Because of this a Flood Watch is \nin effect for these areas from 3 PM - 11 PM. With the \naforementioned shear some storms could be rotating, which could \nlead to an isolated hail threat and a more widespread severe \nwind threat. Model soundings also show a nose of dry air around \n400 mb which would act to enhance downdrafts through \nentrainment. Because of all of these factors some storms may be \ncapable of wind gusts 70-80 mph. There is also a small tornado \nthreat as well with 0-3 km helicity values 150-250 m^2/s^2. The \nbest environment for a tornado looks to be along the \ninternational border and over Sonora, but confidence is low in \nany tornado developing.\n\nOvernight activity looks limited with just some showers over\nGraham and Pinal Counties after midnight. This should allow the\natmosphere to reset and be ready for another round of scattered\nthunderstorms Wednesday afternoon and evening. Mid-level winds\ndecreases slightly compared to today with 500 mb winds 15-20\nkts leading to 0-6 km shear generally 20-30 kts. Plenty of\ninstability will be in place along with seasonable PWAT values.\nThis will lead to a threat of strong to severe winds gusts from\nTucson south and eastward. Locally heavy rainfall leading to \nflash flooding will also be a threat with HREF 1 inch in 1 hr \nprobabilities 30-60 percent south and east of Tucson and 30 \npercent between Sells and Three Points. Past rainfall from today\nand Monday will lead to easier runoff in Cochise and Santa Cruz\nCounties with area washes and rivers seeing faster responses \nfrom rainfall. \n\nLooking ahead through the weekend expect daily afternoon and\nevening thunderstorms from central Pima County eastward with\ntypical monsoon threats of isolated severe winds and heavy\nrainfall leading to flash flooding. Areas that receive repeat\nheavy rainfall each day will be especially susceptible to flash\nflooding. The most likely areas where this will happen will be\nSanta Cruz and Cochise Counties. There will likely be some day \nto day variability. Right now Saturday stands out as a day that\ncould see enhanced storm coverage and strength due to 500 mb \nwinds increasing to 25-30 kts. Medium confidence in winds being\nthis strong even though its a few days out with ensemble \nprobabilities for winds greater than 20 kts at 500 mb 40-60 \npercent Saturday.\n\nHigh temperatures will be 2-7 degrees above normal through the\nweekend.",
